nspawnmgr (0.4.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Multilingual UI: the entire interface (every template, JS-built message,
    server-side validation/error message, and the Guacamole/database
    settings form) is now available in English, Spanish, French,
    Portuguese, Japanese, and Simplified Chinese, auto-detected from the
    browser's Accept-Language header with a per-user override on the
    Profile page. The administrator's guide is translated into all five
    non-English languages too. Protocol/technology names (SSH, RDP, VNC,
    NSPAWN, PODMAN, QEMU, and similar) are deliberately left untranslated
    throughout, as universal technical terms.
  * New host packaging: RPM (Fedora/RHEL, via dnf/rpmbuild), a plain Arch
    Linux package, and a SteamOS-specific variant (relocates all storage
    under /home for the Deck's small root partition, works around SteamOS's
    default firewalld/Gaming Mode quirks) - alongside the existing .deb.
  * Files browsing now works for QEMU VMs and any SSH-enabled Host, not just
    managed nspawn/podman containers - goes over SFTP against the target's
    own SSH server, with a per-session credential prompt (never stored) and
    the connected username shown next to Disconnect.
  * Package upgrades (upgrade-nspawnmgr.sh / a package manager's own
    upgrade) now fully reconcile the self-hosted machine's contents (JRE,
    WARs, guacd, systemd units, SSH credentials) on every run, not just a
    narrow WAR refresh - closing a real gap where a release needing a
    host-side change had no upgrade path short of a full reinstall.
  * Numerous smaller reliability fixes (package-download false-failures,
    dnsmasq/DHCP races on Arch-family installs, host-address/loopback
    diagnostics accuracy, and more). See the project's git history for the
    full list.

 -- nspawnmgr packaging <packaging@nspawnmgr.invalid>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +1000

nspawnmgr (0.3.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Podman and QEMU join systemd-nspawn as full container/VM backends: pods
    (podman, sharing the same nspawnbr0 bridge via netavark host-local IPAM)
    and QEMU/KVM virtual machines (configurable CPU/memory/NIC/pointer
    device, ISO boot media, disk cloning from a template), both alongside
    nspawn containers on one unified Machines page.
  * Redesigned UI: a persistent sidebar/topbar shell, a card-based Machines
    grid with backend badges and inline SSH/RDP/VNC/Files pills, and a
    Profile page with working logout.
  * Machine ownership and sharing are now enforced uniformly across every
    kind (nspawn, podman, QEMU, and admin-managed Hosts) - nothing is
    visible by default except to its owner, an admin, or someone it's been
    explicitly shared with; admins can take ownership of anything.
  * A Host's RUNNING/STOPPED state is now resolved live via a cached TCP
    reachability check instead of always showing RUNNING.
  * Numerous smaller reliability fixes. See the project's git history for
    the full list.

 -- nspawnmgr packaging <packaging@nspawnmgr.invalid>  Tue, 25 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +1000

nspawnmgr (0.2.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Substantial accumulation of fixes and features since the initial 0.1.0
    packaging, including: per-container RDP/VNC provisioning fixes across
    Fedora, Arch, and Debian; an optional PAM-based local-login bypass
    (pam_nspawnmgr) for containers whose own /etc/shadow auth is broken; a
    self-hosted deployment mode (nspawnmgr manages its own Tomcat/guacd
    inside a container it owns); refined container sharing/ownership and
    container-user management, including choosing which account SSH/RDP/VNC
    connect as; and numerous smaller reliability fixes. See the project's
    git history for the full list.

 -- nspawnmgr packaging <packaging@nspawnmgr.invalid>  Sat, 15 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +1000

nspawnmgr (0.1.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Initial .deb packaging: nspawnmgr.war + auth.war deployed into an
    existing Tomcat 9, sudo-capable nspawnmgr_exec service account with
    NOPASSWD-scoped sudoers for routine container lifecycle operations,
    admin/user roles, and the two-mode (stored-secret / admin-approval)
    container-creation gate.

